{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792250000000-engineer-6a-discovery-numbering.report.md collab/numbering-ref/review-r4.md; cat docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md; rg --files collab/numbering-ref; git status --short; git log --oneline master..HEAD'","aggregated_output":"# Engineer report: phase 6a cursor-cell numbering by discovery order\n\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-12 +07. Worktree `datomworld-srvm-p6a`, branch\n`srvm-phase6a`, HEAD `d77da9f0`. **Nothing committed.** Implements\n`collab/numbering-ref/note.md` §§1–6, with the owner answers of §7\n(the occurrence count stays in the key; the `:work` default is 4194304).\n\n## Step 0: merge NOT done\n\nIn this `-p` session, `git merge master` (every form) and `git ls-tree`\nneeded approval that couldn't be granted, so the branch is **unmerged**.\nImpact:\n\n- Code: master changes nothing under `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/` or\n  `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (I checked with `git diff --stat\n  HEAD...master`), so the code and test changes should merge cleanly.\n- Docs: master's `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` (167 lines) and\n  `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` (42 lines) carry the\n  cursor-cell rename. The branch's copies are the older text. So I applied\n  A1–A9 to **master's** text and left the branch's doc files untouched (see\n  \"Docs\" below).\n- Baseline before any change, unmerged branch: `rows-test` plus\n  `frame-test`, 31 tests, 554 assertions, 0 failures.\n\n**Orchestrator action:** merge master, then `git apply\ncollab/1792250000000-docs-amendments-against-master.patch`, then `cp\ncollab/numbering-ref/note.md\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`. A1, A2,\nA4 and A7 link to that note by name, and it isn't in `docs/design` on\nmaster yet. A `/dev/null` diff for a new-file hunk was blocked. The note's\nStatus line still says \"not yet implemented\"; flip it at landing.\n\n## Files changed\n\n| File | Added | Deleted |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` | 536 | 178 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` | 615 | 32 |\n\nDocs, outside the tree (apply after the merge):\n`collab/1792250000000-docs-amendments-against-master.patch` (545 lines),\nplus the full amended files `collab/1792250000000-carrier.amended.md` and\n`collab/1792250000000-continuation.amended.md`.\n\n**rows.cljc against the note's estimate (about 280 added, 135 deleted).**\nThe procedure region (lines 221–700, from the section comment up to\n`emit-numbered`) is 480 lines with blanks, about 420 non-blank. That\nmatches the probe's 405 non-blank lines for the same region (note\nappendix A). The note's 280 was a planning estimate it called \"not a\ndiff\". The numstat also counts as moved: `emit-numbered`, `emit` and\n`default-budget` (moved above `emit-node`), `emit-slot` and `emit-node`\nre-indented for the path and child-numberer arguments, and the `decode*`\ntranslation. About 100 of the added lines are docstrings and comments.\n\n### What changed in rows.cljc\n\n- **Deleted:** `check-label-bytes!`; all of 6a's `canonical-numbering`\n  body (label seeding, `checked-*`, `ce-slot`/`ce`, `positions`,\n  `classes`, `refine`, `search`); `:label-bytes` and `:search` in\n  `default-budget`; the duplicate `(declare canonical-numbering)`.\n- **Added**, porting the probe's procedure, not importing it:\n  - `work!`;\n  - `charge-representation!`: iterative, explicit stack, aborts against\n    the remaining budget, keeps 6a's `#?(:cljd (.-length x) :default\n    (alength x))`;\n  - `key-bytes!`: charge, then `cbor/encode`, then 6a's `bytes-key`;\n  - `sort-by-key!`;\n  - `depth-scan!`: explicit stack of lazy element sequences; each\n    position is charged when popped;\n  - `walk-cells`, `item-cells`, `body-ranks`, `new-run`, `meet!`,\n    `enter-slot!`, `enter-node!`, `unordered!`, `fixpoint!`,\n    `unnumbered-cells`;\n  - `tie-failure`: the probe's `group-failure`, with its `for` replaced\n    by `reduce`;\n  - `resolve!` (without the diagnostic `:permissive` switch and trace),\n    `key-walk` (the probe's `local-run`), `spell-cell`, `spell-slot`,\n    `spell-node`, `discovery-numbering`, `unresolved!`, `child-numberer`.\n- **Rewritten:** `canonical-numbering`, with the same 1- and 2-arity\n  contract and an empty census still returning `{}`; `default-budget`\n  (`:work 4194304`); `descriptors` (synthetic `:entry` → `[:V :V]`);\n  `emit-slot` and `emit-node` (they thread a row path and a child\n  numberer); `emit` and `emit-numbered`; the `decode*` recompute (the\n  translation wraps the whole `emit` call); the namespace docstring.\n- **Gate order** is as the note fixes it: census pair count, then the\n  iterative depth scan, then ranks (charged encodings), counts, the walk\n  (which also checks its own depth), the tie test, the refusal, and\n  totality.\n\n## Tests\n\n| Run (JVM only, all foreground) | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `clj -M:kondo --lint` on both changed files | 0 errors, 0 warnings |\n| `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`, slow included | 30 tests, 461 assertions, 0 failures (about 23 s) |\n| `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*' -n yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test` | 688 tests, 12299 assertions, 0 failures |\n\nThe 688-test run was made before the incidence-family generator was added\nto `rows_test`. `rows-test` was re-run in full afterwards (461 assertions,\nthe row above). `rows.cljc` was restored byte-identical (checked with\n`diff`) after the mutation runs. Node and Dart were not run, per the\nbrief.\n\n`rows_test` has 30 deftests (6a: 21). One is `^:slow` and wrapped in\n`dao.test-slow/guard`.\n\n**Kept unchanged:** `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n`window-sorted-and-unique`, `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n`alias-topology-and-census`, `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n`child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`,\n`pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells`, the three marker tests,\n`cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`, and the\nseven-cell fixture.\n\n**Changed beyond the note's list:** `schema-and-literal-canonicality`\n(see deviation 9).\n\n**Extended:** `numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (item 20).\n\n**Rewritten per §4:**\n- `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (also carries item 17)\n- `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`\n- `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-not-spelled` (also carries\n  item 21's 400000-literal-in-a-set assertions)\n\n**New, one per §5 item:**\n\n| Item | Test |\n|---|---|\n| 12 | `discovery-order-numbering` |\n| 13 | `occurrence-count-separates-members` |\n| 14 | `interchangeable-ties` |\n| 15 | `alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key` |\n| 16 | `unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse` |\n| 18 | `counterexample-search-quick` (fast) and `counterexample-search` (`^:slow`) |\n| 19 | `numbering-is-total` |\n| 21 | `gates-and-work` |\n\n**Generator results** (portable Park–Miller seed, no `java.util.Random`).\nNo failure means 0 root or refusal mismatches against a renamed and\nshuffled copy, and 0 decode or re-emit failures.\n\n| Generator | Quick | Slow |\n|---|---|---|\n| random values | 250 states | 2000: 1978 numbered, 22 refused, max `:work` 776001 |\n| frame-bearing | 40 | 200: 197 numbered, 3 refused |\n| with children | 20 | 100: 99 numbered, 1 refused |\n| incidence families | 100 | 1000: 832 numbered, 168 refused |\n\nThe quick test takes about 1.5 s on the JVM.\n\n## Pins reproduced (no re-pinning)\n\nEvery pin and numbering the note names reproduced at the first run:\n\n| Pin | State | 6a root? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fab223…` | `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` | yes |\n| `c4a7c7…` | worked frame | yes |\n| `2d9873a8…` | review counterexample, `y cc-1, x cc-2` | yes |\n| `07eda35d…` | `#{X Y}` | yes |\n| `4993b5b1…` | `[x x y]` | yes |\n| `536c810b…` | `[x y y]` | yes |\n| `da69c679…` | `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` | yes |\n| `49d4478c…` | `{X 1, Y 1}` | yes |\n| `387f294a…` | codex A, both enumerations | yes |\n| `be62ba43…` | codex B | yes |\n| `18b70de6…` | codex C | yes |\n| `a850fce6…` | `[#{X Y} X]` | yes |\n| `0193312…` | witness, `b cc-1, a cc-2` | yes |\n| `6e7c7a6b…` | ten cells | yes |\n| `e67405ec…` | `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | no (new) |\n| `5376f133…` | balanced-alias witness, both enumerations | no (new) |\n| `d84480d4…` | `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` | no (new) |\n| `726a9fbb…` | path | no (new) |\n| `0b9ef61e…` | frame fixture 1, `y cc-1, x cc-2, :test/cursor cc-3` | no (new) |\n| `ff124bfc…` | frame fixture 2, same numbering | no (new) |\n\nEvery pin is asserted with its full id.\n\n**Refusals reproduced:** the paths and cells of appendix A for all eleven\nrefused states, including `[15 0 :member]` and the install child\n`[12 a 15]`. The latter decodes to `[<root> 12 a 15]` with cells\n`[cc-1 cc-2]`.\n\n**Removed:** `55ff168e…` (the loop plus 2-cycle now refuses at path\n`[15]`, cells `[:a :b]`).\n\n## Mutation results\n\nEach mutant was a temporary source edit, restored from a copy and checked\nwith `diff`.\n\n| # | Mutant | Caught by |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Drop the occurrence count from `[:u j r m]` | **Caught**: pins in `canonical-roots-are-pinned`, `discovery-order-numbering`, `occurrence-count-separates-members`, `alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key` (17 failures, 6 errors). The generator finds no mismatch: it is a different, still canonical rule, as the fourth review found. |\n| 2 | Drop the first-occurrence index | **Caught**: the balanced-alias witness gives two accepted roots for its two enumerations (`rows_test` `alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`), plus a pin and a refusal assertion. The generator alone misses it, as the review predicted. |\n| 3a | Reverse the host order in which a *passing* tie group is entered | **Survives, as it should**: the note's §2 proves this order cannot change the rows. Every pin and the 3300-state generator are unchanged. An equivalent mutant, so no test is possible. |\n| 3b | Enter a *failing* group in host order instead of refusing (the probe's `:permissive`) | **Caught**: 39 failures in the refusal tests. With the incidence families the generator also catches it on its own (20 invariance failures in 1000 slow states, 6 in the 100 quick ones). Without the incidence families the generator missed it, which is why I added them (deviation 11). |\n| 4 | Skip the confinement clause (iii) only | **Caught** by the three shared-across-groups fixtures, and by the generator through the incidence families (9 slow, 3 quick). |\n| 5 | Skip the representation charge in `key-bytes!` | **Caught** by the large-literal `[:work]` assertions (3 failures). |\n| 5b | Skip the depth scan's per-position charge | **Caught**: the `:work` counter is no longer 2001 under `{:work 2000}`, and the default and 900000 cases no longer refuse. |\n| 6 | Validate frames before the numbering recomputation in `decode*` | **Caught** by both halves of `numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (3 failures). |\n\n## Deviations from the note, and why\n\n1. **No merge** (above).\n2. **Docs come as a patch against master's text**, not as edits to the\n   branch's stale copies. Editing those would conflict with master's rename.\n3. **A1 adaptation.** Note §1 went into carrier §6 as named paragraphs. Its\n   section references were renamed: §1.x to the paragraph names, and §2,\n   §3 and the appendix to \"numbering note §…\". \"carrier §N\" became \"§N\".\n   The implementation pointers (`rows.cljc:NNN`, \"6a's gate…\", \"6a's\n   test…\") and the appendix's measured figures were dropped from the\n   normative spec, because they go stale. The rule paragraph was kept as\n   §6's summary. A3 to A9 were applied verbatim. A9 appends items 12–22\n   after item 11.\n4. **`emit-numbered` takes an optional `number-child`** argument, `(fn\n   [child path] numbering)`, defaulting to canonical numbering. Without\n   it, item 16's install-child carrier can't be built: `emit-numbered`\n   numbers children canonically, and the child would refuse on the lift\n   side. Test-facing only, and the 2-arity is unchanged.\n5. **`canonical-numbering` has a 3-arity** `[task budget counter]`, so\n   tests can observe the `:work` counter (2001) and `:estimator-steps`.\n   The refusal ex-data stays exactly `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/kind\n   :yin.k/path}`, plus `:yin.k/cursor-cells` for the tie refusal. There\n   is no probe-only diagnostic key.\n6. **Install children are numbered under the caller's budget.** 6a's\n   `emit-node` used the default budget. The child refusal path is\n   threaded through nested installs: a grandchild reads `[12 a 12 b …]`,\n   where the probe reset it to the child-local path.\n7. **An empty census returns `{}`** right after the census gate, before\n   the depth scan. This is 6a's contract and the note §4's (\"empty census\n   → `{}`\"); the probe walked anyway.\n8. **Cell lists in refusals and defects are sorted by `str`**, not by\n   `compare`, for portability. The order is the same for every keyword\n   id used.\n9. **`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, a kept test, needed two carriers\n   built with `emit-numbered`.** The lift now refuses those two states\n   itself: an unreachable census cell is the §1.6 totality defect, and\n   `[x [1]]` / `[x (1)]` tie and share `x`. The test checks decode's own\n   `:extra-cursor-cell` and `:duplicate-key`, so it now supplies the\n   numbering. The note §4 didn't foresee this.\n10. **The 400000-literal-in-a-set assertions** (default budget, `{:work\n    2000}` with counter 2001 and 0 estimator steps, `{:work 900000}` with\n    between 1 and 999 estimator steps) live only in\n    `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-not-spelled`. Duplicating\n    them in `gates-and-work` would double the cost of the heaviest test.\n11. **The generator gained the fourth review's incidence families**\n    (quick 100, slow 1000) beyond item 18's three generators, so the\n    generator alone catches mutants 3b and 4.\n12. **`decode*` with `:check-numbering false`** (used by\n    `canonical-under`) still runs the recomputation, as 6a did, so it can\n    surface the translated tie refusal. This is unchanged 6a behaviour,\n    noted for the record.\n\n## Uncertain, or for the orchestrator\n\n- **Node and Dart are unrun.** Portability measures taken:\n  - no `for`;\n  - explicit stacks for the depth scan and the estimator;\n  - no `@#'`;\n  - no multi-key `assoc` on nil;\n  - no unary minus;\n  - catches use `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`;\n  - the test PRNG keeps every product below 2^53.\n\n  `test/cljd-out` holds stale generated tests for the old\n  `rows-test`; per build-n-test, `rm -rf test/cljd-out` before trusting\n  a plain `cljd test`.\n- **Possible slow tests on Node and Dart.**\n  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-not-spelled` takes 1.9 s on\n  the JVM: it scans 800k positions three times and builds several\n  400000-element literals. The quick generator takes about 1.5 s on the\n  JVM. If either goes over about 5 s on a lane, tag it `^:slow` and wrap\n  it in `guard`.\n- **Mutant 3's wording.** \"Make a tie order depend on host enumeration\"\n  was run both ways (3a, 3b). Only the unsafe form (3b) is a real defect.\n- **`:work` figures differ from appendix A** (max 776001 here against\n  625518 there). The generators use a different RNG, so the states\n  differ. The 2001 and fewer-than-1000-estimator-step thresholds hold.\n# Cursor-cell numbering review r4 — base design only\n\nReviewed HEAD `1bb2638cfcf00e509f34bf463e3083e51d593697`, the uncommitted round-4 note, report and base probe. Phase 6a reference: `d77da9f04276fca34db6fe6c36bea38a576c09b2`. Foreground review, no agents. Only this findings file was edited; experimental mutations used temporary in-memory Var replacements, restored in `finally`. JVM commands ran from `collab/numbering-probe`, supplying scripts on stdin to `mise exec -- java -Xmx1g` or `-Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -`.\n\n**No accepted-state canonicality counterexample was found.** The exact corrected independent 5,000-state generator numbered 4,110 states and structurally refused 890, with zero root or refusal-path mismatches over two independently renamed/shuffled copies per state. Independent exhaustive bijection checks verified 463 member swaps in 427 passing root-run tie groups. The base tie rule has a sound argument; D6's former recursive-search proof problem does not apply to this design.\n\n**Changes are still required for the resource gates and the probe's decoder ordering.** Comparison encoding now happens after a conservative byte charge, but the occurrence scan can overflow the host stack before the depth gate, and the representation estimator dropped 6a's early limit check. The probe also performs frame validation before discovery recomputation, so the unchanged 6a test run does not validate the claimed new decoder order.\n\nReferences: `note` = `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`; `probe` = `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`; `rows` and `rows_test` = phase 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`.\n\n| Severity | Location | Finding and required correction |\n|---|---|---|\n| **HIGH** | note:164–177, 249–256, 337–343; probe:121–150, 184–187, 392–403 | `occurrence-counts` runs the recursive `walk-cells` before the walk's depth check. With one reachable cell inside 3,000 nested vectors and `{:depth 3}`, numbering throws `StackOverflowError`, not `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:depth]`. The 4- and 300-level variants give the intended refusal. Enforce structural depth before recursive scans, memo-key hashing and spelling preparation can descend unchecked; carry depth through the scans or use a bounded iterative traversal. Add the deep adversarial case, not just `[[[[X]]]]`. The same discipline is needed for nested literal/descriptor representation sizing. |\n| **MEDIUM** | note:168–178, 321–323; probe:94–110 | `rep-bound` is not the stated surviving 6a `size`: it removed the `remaining` argument and all intermediate limit checks. Under `{:work 2000}`, a set containing a 400,000-scalar literal invokes the estimator **400,012 times** before returning `[:work]`. CBOR allocation is correctly avoided, but the budget does not bound this preparation work or its recursion. Restore a remaining-budget-aware, early-aborting estimator, and qualify the accounting claim to distinguish encoded bytes, representation nodes and actual host heap bytes. A comparison byte vector and encoder intermediates consume more heap than the CBOR payload length; the representation bound is a conservative abstract charge, not a measured byte-for-byte heap ceiling. |\n| **MEDIUM** | note:149–160, appendix A claim of exact implementation; probe:484–502 | The wrapper calls the entire old `rows/decode*`, including `validate-frames`, before doing discovery recomputation. The existing `numbering-precedes-frame-defect` witness, decoded with `p/decode*`, returns `:reserved-name` rather than `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`. Fix the probe or explicitly identify this second decoder limitation and add an actual discovery ordering test. The proposed production insertion around rows:570–575 is correctly placed before frame validation; preserve it rather than copying this wrapper literally. |\n| **LOW** | note:270–274 | The asserted example of equal incomplete keys is stale after occurrence counts were added. A standalone `#{[A B] [B A]}` has counts 2,2; `#{[A B] [C D]}` has counts 1,1,1,1, and its disjoint members pass the local tie test. These are not the claimed equal residue keys. Replace the illustration with equal-size, equal-degree unresolved incidence structures, or remove that comparison. The incomplete-key clause itself remains necessary. |\n| **LOW** | note:208–209, 249–256 | Make the termination measure precise for the queue as well as the current item. The prose explicitly allows a tie step that numbers no cell but replaces queued members with smaller sub-terms; that does not decrease the displayed pair when the walk's item remains the same. A multiset measure on pending subtree sizes, together with unnumbered-cell count and the explicit phase transitions, covers that case. Alternatively restrict that branch to invalid duplicate values and state the valid-input premise. This is a repairable proof presentation issue, not the former D6 recursion gap. |\n| **LOW** | revise3 report, “What is refused, and why” | “The rest were harmless ties” is stronger than the measurements. The tables leave 60 refusals undecided (59 incidence-family states and one child state). Sampling equal roots is not proof of interchangeability. Preserve that distinction in the owner summary. |\n\n## 1. Canonicality and the independent generator\n\nI reused the corrected r3 script's definitions through `gen`, with seed `179221`, unchanged. Each iteration constructs the same two independent renamed/shuffled copies as r3, including all sets, maps and censuses. The removed D5/D6 comparisons consumed no randomness, so this preserves the original generator/copy sequence. Refusal classification uses a **separate** RNG (`9123`) and does not perturb that sequence.\n\nFor each state I compared the base `p/do-root` result across the three copies. Successful results must be the same root. Structural refusals were normalized to kind and row path, excluding source-name diagnostics. There were no resource refusals in this corpus.\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---:|\n| States / install-child states | 5,000 / 250 |\n| Numbered | 4,110 |\n| Structurally refused | 890 |\n| Root or acceptance/refusal/path mismatches | **0** |\n| Refused: exhaustively interchangeable | 470 |\n| Refused: demonstrated non-interchangeable | 100 |\n| Refused: sampled equal, undecided | 320 |\n| Decode successes among first 100 states | 60 |\n| Decode resource refusals in that subset | 14 |\n| Other decode failures in that subset | **0** |\n\nThe first-100 decode check is a separate rerun preserving the same generator/copy sequence. The other 26 states were structurally refused. Resource refusals are the retained 6a recomputation limitation; successful valid carriers were checked through the base wrapper.\n\nMy refusal classifier does not call `p/classify-refused`, `p/reorder`, the author's permutations or renamer. It traverses the schema independently, transforms children before their parent, and records each guest set/map's member count in that post-order. It then takes the Cartesian product of independently generated permutations when the product is at most 720. Reordering each original task in the same post-order makes the container ordinals stable even when parents are shuffled. Above 720, it uses 48 independent shuffled arrangements. Permissive emission is used only to measure roots. Two different roots prove non-interchangeability; one root from exhaustive enumeration certifies the tested host-order invariance; one root from sampling stays undecided.\n\nTo attack positive tie-test results independently, I temporarily wrapped `group-failure`. On each passing **root-run** group, I collected the unnumbered cell IDs directly from its member data, enumerated bijections between the first member's cells and each other member's cells, extended each candidate to a two-way swap, and tested the entire task under that swap. My equality projection treats guest sets as mathematical sets, maps as maps, census as an ID-to-body map, and ordered rows as vectors. It retains bodies, aliasing, fixed numbered cells and all ordered occurrences. All **463 pair checks in 427 passing groups** found a whole-task automorphism. Some passing groups occur in a root run which eventually refuses because of another group; the check covers those too. This supplements, rather than substitutes for, the accepted-root comparison.\n\nAn earlier exploratory run sampled 16 permissive copies using the generator's own RNG and obtained 4,100/900. That altered later generated states. It is not the exact r3-sequence rerun and is not counted in the table above.\n\n### Assessment of section 2\n\nThe root tie step is sound: equal **complete** spellings provide an index-matched bijection preserving ordered structure, unordered membership, literal data, body rank, occurrence count and already-numbered cells. Clauses (ii) and (iii), after the fixpoint, ensure that every swapped unnumbered cell is confined to its one member. Extending that bijection to a block permutation therefore preserves the whole state. Swapping blocks changes private source-to-number maps but preserves emitted rows. This is precisely the confinement obligation that the r1 orbit argument lacked.\n\nWithin a key walk, confinement applies to the item being keyed, not to the whole root. That is sufficient for a canonical key: the item also reads the global occurrence counts and body ranks as invariant cell colors. Equal complete spellings preserve those colors. The local first-occurrence index preserves the alias relation; omitting it is unsound, as the targeted mutant below demonstrates.\n\nCounts are structure-derived because the scan counts schema positions, not distinct host objects or source IDs; it excludes the census and separately scoped child tasks. Shuffling unordered containers cannot change those counts. Body ranks derive from sorted distinct structural labels. The first-occurrence index derives from the key walk's canonical decisions, with nested walks starting at the enclosing local counter. Its validity follows from the smaller-subterm key induction plus the interchangeable-tie argument, not from an assumed host-independent raw encounter order.\n\nUnlike D6, a nested key request is over an actual smaller member subterm. There are no synthetic component or forced-trial transitions to justify. Fixpoint passes that repeat number a cell; passing nonempty cell-bearing tie groups also number cells. A finite queue/subterm argument covers the remaining no-new-cell branch. The displayed termination measure needs the clarification listed above, but I found no infinite loop or base canonicality defect.\n\nDo not describe this test as uniquely “the cheapest sound test” without a cost model. Nor does the seven-cell example prove that **every** sound rule needs search: richer deterministic structural analysis can distinguish a triangle from a square. It proves that arbitrary tied-edge entry is unsafe and that this chosen cheap test correctly refuses it. That is enough for this base design.\n\n## 2. Mutants\n\nAll mutants ran without source edits. The first four were rerun over the exact saved 5,000-state corpus and its two copies. The byte-charge mutant used the targeted large-literal budget fixture.\n\n| Mutation | Rerun result | What catches it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Drop occurrence count using `:count-spelling false` | 2,649 numbered, 2,351 refused; **0 invariance mismatches**; 2,073 changed roots among states numbered by both versions | Count-specific fixture pins and coverage expectations. This is a legitimate alternative rule with worse coverage/different roots, not inherently a canonicality failure. |\n| Enter failing ties in host order using `:permissive true` | 5,000 numbered; **86 root mismatches** | Renaming/shuffling invariance. This switch affects key walks as well as the root. |\n| Skip confinement by suppressing only `:shared-across-groups` failures | 4,496 numbered, 504 refused; **42 mismatches** | Renaming/shuffling invariance. Keep the complete-key and within-group checks intact for this mutation. |\n| Drop local first-occurrence index from `[:u j rank count]` | 3,998 numbered, 1,002 refused; **0 invariance mismatches in this generator**; 388 changed roots among states numbered by both versions | Corpus pins catch a change, but this generator alone misses the unsoundness. The targeted balanced-alias witness below gives two accepted roots. |\n| Skip representation-byte charge in `key-bytes!` | Huge-literal fixture numbers under `{:work 2000}`, charged work **44**, whereas base refuses `[:work]` | The large-literal budget assertion. A canonicality-only rerun does not test accounting. |\n\nThe balanced-alias witness is\n\n```clojure\n#{[X X Y Y] [Z W Z W]}\n```\n\nAll four cells have equal bodies and occurrence count 2. The base gives one root under reversal, `5376f133…`. Dropping the index makes both complete keys equal and the disjoint/confinement clauses pass, although the alias patterns differ. Reversing the set's enumeration then changes the accepted root from `5376f133…` to `09115434…`. Add this witness to the alias-pattern acceptance test; pair-edge-only examples do not adequately test the index component when count spelling is present.\n\n## 3. Byte/work accounting and gates\n\nThe former principal encoding defect is fixed: every comparison encoding in the numbering procedure goes through `key-bytes!`, which calls `spend!` on the representation bound before calling `bytes-key`. Sorting caches encoded keys rather than repeatedly encoding from the comparator. Synthetic cell/tag/tie overhead is included in the bound of the eventual encoded spelling; spelling positions are charged as they are constructed. Input literals are retained by reference in spellings.\n\nIndependent instrumentation and gate checks:\n\n| Case | Observation |\n|---|---|\n| 400,000-scalar literal inside a set, default budget | `[:work]`; only the 135-byte body-label encoding occurred before refusal, **no huge-literal encoding** |\n| Same set under `{:work 2000}` | `[:work]`, but estimator entered 400,012 times before refusal |\n| Same set with byte charge removed | Accepted; work 44 |\n| Two cells, descriptor length 128 | Work 3,689; charged comparison encodings total 367 bytes |\n| Two cells, descriptor length 65,536 | Work 265,321; comparison encodings total 65,778 bytes; accepted |\n| 2, 65 and 10,000 cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` | All refuse `[:cursor-cells]` |\n| One cell, 3,000 or 10,000 nested vectors, `{:depth 3}` | `StackOverflowError` before the depth gate |\n| Same vector construction at depth 4 or 300 | Intended `[:depth]` refusal |\n| Nested singleton sets at depths 40, 80, 120, 180 and 240 | All stop at `[:work]` under default budgets |\n| Empty census, singleton set holding the huge literal | `[:work]`, rather than the old numbering procedure's immediate `{}` |\n\nThe descriptor measurement is my two-cell/single-body fixture, not the report's four-cell/two-body fixture; their absolute work totals need not match. It confirms that descriptor bytes are now charged and that local rank spellings do not repeat the large descriptor.\n\nThe census gate is before ranks/counts/walking, as promised, but **after** building the `bodies` map (probe:392–393). If “first” is intended to prevent oversized census preparation allocation too, check the raw pair count before that materialization. This is also a residual in 6a, whose label preparation precedes its gate; retaining the old line is not by itself evidence of preallocation protection.\n\nHashing memo keys containing member data can also scan large input literals before a byte charge. The implementation should make the accounting promise about bounded, charged preparation explicit, rather than calling every read/allocation paid. In particular, the estimator's docstring “allocating nothing” is literally false for `str` and lazy `mapcat` scaffolding, though those are not the old giant-CBOR allocation defect.\n\nThe nested-singleton result is another practical cost to report: fairly shallow identity-bearing structure can exhaust the new work budget even with one cell. It is a resource refusal, not a structural tie refusal or canonicality failure. No polynomial claim is needed, but a production performance assessment should include this shape.\n\n## 4. Refusal predicate and decode\n\nThe operational predicate in §3 matches `resolve!`: re-key queued groups to a no-progress pass; refuse if **any** group is incomplete, overlaps internally, or shares an unnumbered cell with another remaining group. If every group passes, enter them and return to the fixpoint. Key walks leave residue rather than refusing. `group-failure` implements the three clauses in the listed priority; root refusal reports the first failing group's path and all failing groups' unnumbered cells. The corrected mixed-body and anchored examples no longer overstate what an ordered anchor certifies.\n\nThe incomplete-key illustration needs the count-aware correction above. The operational iff should also be read subject to resource limits and valid input: a budget can stop the procedure before it reaches a structural decision, and an unreachable census cell raises a totality defect after a successful walk.\n\nInstall translation is fixed and independently verified. A parent with install `'a` holding the 2-cycle gives lift refusal `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[12 a 15]`. A phase-6a-emitted carrier for that parent decoded through discovery gives `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, carried cells `[:yin.k/cc-1 :yin.k/cc-2]`. Work-limit refusals at lift and decode under `{:work 1}` remain `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`, untranslated.\n\nThe whole-discovery-emission catch correctly includes children and catches only the specified lift-side refusal. The separate frame-order defect is real: the prototype's retained decoder already validates frames before the catch/recomputation. Run the rewritten ordering test against the discovery decoder, not merely the unchanged copied rows namespace.\n\n## 5. Section 4 audit and portability\n\nThe probe's rows and rows_test files match the phase-6a worktree by SHA-1:\n\n- rows: `e02350337c1b48a8d02901e46499d4cd0ab90f19`\n- rows_test: `d8aeed70b39a1f4770ba99fb699d06e6561a4128`\n\nThe named deletion/rewrite spans are accurate: label-bound helper 212–230, checked wrappers 245–251, label seed 252–255, encoding 257–284, positions/depth 285–325, classes/refinement/search 326–354, emission 181–197 and 358–372, decoder recomputation 570–575 followed by frame validation 576–578. The rewritten test locations 171–178, 307–328, 400–403 and 406–421 are also correct. The unreachable `base` assertion is now explicitly rewritten as a totality defect; reachable/custody literal cases stay valid.\n\nThe cut list correctly retains the census gate and relocates the depth gate, adds the synthetic entry descriptor, threads child paths, and distinguishes the two `bytes-key` signatures. Add the depth/preparation and early-bound corrections to that brief; the listed four-level depth fixture cannot detect the current failure.\n\nPortable `.cljc` concerns are accurately identified: replace the probe's `for` in `group-failure` for potentially long sequences, keep 6a's byte-array-taking `bytes-key` with an explicit charged encode call, and remove reflection used only by the scratch emitter/decoder. The JVM `^bytes`/`alength` representation branch also needs the existing host-conditional implementation from 6a. Recursion depth must protect **all** preparatory traversals; guarding only the main walk does not establish safe bounded recursion on any of the three hosts.\n\nPhase 6a's unchanged tests passed independently: **21 tests, 324 assertions, zero failures/errors**. This validates the copied reference, not a production integration or a discovery decoder test suite. No Node or Dart execution was performed.\n\n## 6. Size, difficulty and refusal policy\n\nThe procedure range 79–416 has **305 nonblank lines**, or **296 after excluding comment-only lines**. The comparable 6a helpers have approximately 136 nonblank/noncomment lines. Thus the report's underlying size comparison is credible, although “non-blank” is an imprecise label for its counting rule. Roughly 200 added/replacement rows lines versus 135 deleted is a reasonable planning estimate, not an implemented diff. Correct scan/depth/accounting and decode validation add some work beyond that estimate.\n\nThe base is conceptually simpler than D6 and avoids 6a's label growth and individualization search. It still requires recursive local walks, provisional and root numbering, memoization sensitive to numbering/counter, faithful alias spellings, deferred worklists, confinement scans, resource accounting and decoder integration. It is **larger code and a substantial implementation**, not merely sorting a vector once. The proof burden is better contained than D6's, but “one page” does not make the boundary/accounting obligations disappear. I would not promise a shorter implementation/review schedule from these measurements.\n\nThe measured random refusal rate is **0.95%** (19/2000); frame-bearing is **2%** (4/200). These are generator rates, not production evidence. The independent tie-heavy corpus refuses **17.8%**, including 470 exhaustively harmless states. Valid small shapes that 6a can carry now deterministically refuse. That is an explicit behavioral tradeoff of the adopted base rule, not a defect that this review recommends repairing with D6.\n\nI cannot certify those rates as acceptable for unrestricted production suspension/migration. They may be acceptable for an explicitly refusal-capable carrier if callers preserve the live task and handle the structured refusal, but the samples cannot establish operational reliability. Treat this as an owner-accepted capability limit pending a real snapshot corpus; do not describe the random/frame samples as demonstrating practical coverage. Retain the occurrence count: it materially improves coverage, and dropping it saves little implementation complexity.\n\nThe owner-selected base remains a defensible implementation direction. Before implementation approval, fix the preparatory depth gate and early budget estimator, reconcile the probe's validator order with the production brief, and correct the small proof/example/report overclaims. No search extension is needed to address these findings.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); implemented in phase 6a (branch srvm-phase6a).** Round 4 of the same day\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\nthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\nthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\nadopted; revised the same day after the fourth review\n(`collab/1792230000000-…review-r4…findings.md`: no canonicality\ncounterexample in 5,000 states; the depth gate moved before every\nrecursive scan, the representation estimator made budget-aware, the\nprobe's decoder put in the production order, and the wording of the\ntermination measure, the incomplete-key example and the refusal figures\ncorrected). Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\ncarrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\nand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\nappendices are not.\n\n**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\nwhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\nis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\nexcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\nthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\nmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\nalready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\ncount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\nmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\nare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\nre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\nproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\nunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\nsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n\n**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\nIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\noccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\nentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\nIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\nthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\nrefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n\n## 1. The rule\n\n**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\nprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\nover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\noutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\nmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\nsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\nmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\nis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\ndescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\norder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\nare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n\n### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n\nWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\nits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\na member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\nthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\nfrom that one numbering):\n\n- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n\nKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR; the spelling is hash-free.\n`[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part\nof its key, and so is each cell's occurrence count, which separates\n`#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search (appendix A).\n\n### 1.2 The walk\n\n**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\nslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\nrepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\nwith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\nkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\nan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\nentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\nentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\nwalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\nwithin one container in key order).\n\n**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\nreceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\nis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\ncensus row stays the contents table.\n\n**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\norder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\ntreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\nre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\nqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\nremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\ntest.\n\n### 1.3 The tie test\n\nA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\nare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\nother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\ncell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\nof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\npasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\nhost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\nreturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\nresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\nthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n\nThis is a sound test, and a cheap one: (i)–(iii) are exactly what the\nproof of §2 needs for the index-matched swap of two members to be an\nautomorphism of the state; whatever it refuses may still be\ninterchangeable (§3 says what), but nothing it accepts can be host-order\ndependent. No claim is made that it is the cheapest such test, or that\nevery sound test needs a search for what it refuses.\n\n### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n\nThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\na member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\nan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\ngroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\nto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\nThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\nThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\ninstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\nthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\nby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\nresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\nsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\nnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n(`rows.cljc:576-578`), so a wrong numbering is reported as\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells` and an unresolved one as\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` even when the task also has a frame defect\n(test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, run against the rewritten\ndecoder with both carriers; the prototype's decoder reproduces this\nplacement, appendix A).\n\n### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n\n- `:cursor-cells`: the census's pair count is charged first, before the\n  census map, ranks, counts or any walk is built (6a's gate at\n  `rows.cljc:256`, kept, moved before the label preparation).\n- `:depth`: enforced second, **before any recursive traversal of the\n  task**, by one bounded iterative scan (an explicit stack, never the\n  host stack) over rows, members, structural and literal data and the\n  census, install children excluded (each is gated by its own run). The\n  depth of a position is its slot steps from the task (a member step\n  counts one, a map entry's key or value two) plus, inside data, its\n  collection nesting; a position deeper than `:depth` refuses\n  `[:depth]`. After the scan every recursive read of the task (cell\n  scans, occurrence counts, memo-key hashing, spelling, encoding) nests\n  at most `:depth` levels, so no input can overflow the host stack\n  before the gate fires: one cell inside 3000 nested vectors under\n  `{:depth 3}` refuses `[:depth]`, as does a literal nested 3000 deep.\n  The walk's own check (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`) is kept as\n  well. Default 256, unchanged.\n- `:work`: one unit per position scanned (the depth scan), visited\n  (walk, key walk, cell scans, count and rank preparation), keyed (one\n  per member per keying, so the number of key walks and memo lookups is\n  charged) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key walk's memo key, and,\n  **before every CBOR encoding made for a comparison** (keys, rank\n  labels, structural keys), the representation bound of the value\n  encoded: 6a's `size` of `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`),\n  128 per node or scalar, 4 per character, 16 plus the length per byte\n  payload, an upper bound of the encoding's bytes. The estimator is\n  iterative and budget-aware: it runs against the budget remaining at\n  the call and stops as soon as its running total exceeds it, so it\n  takes at most `remaining/16` steps on any input and never reads to the\n  end of a value it cannot afford (a set holding a 400000-scalar literal\n  under `{:work 900000}` refuses `[:work]` after 779 estimator steps;\n  under `{:work 2000}` the depth scan refuses at its 2001st position and\n  the estimator is never entered). Spellings hold input literals by\n  reference, so their own size is their spelled positions. What this\n  buys, stated exactly: every comparison encoding is charged before it\n  is made, by a conservative abstract bound of its encoded bytes and of\n  the encoder's intermediate nodes; the encoder's intermediates and the\n  comparison byte vector occupy more host heap than the payload length,\n  so the budget bounds allocation up to a constant factor of the host's\n  representation, not byte for byte, and the input itself is not\n  counted. Preparation is bounded and charged: hashing a memo key reads\n  data the scan has already charged, and the number of such reads is\n  charged. No polynomial bound is claimed; the budget is the bound.\n  Default `4194304`, the scale of the deleted `:label-bytes` (appendix\n  A: at most 625518 over 3300 generated states, 544659 with a\n  65536-character stream descriptor). A literal of more than about 30000\n  scalars inside a guest set or map exceeds it (6a allowed about 8000);\n  in an ordered position a literal is never spelled or encoded and costs\n  one unit per position of its data in the depth scan (a 400000-scalar\n  literal beside a cell numbers at 401323).\n- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted.\n\nExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10\n(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\nverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n\n### 1.6 Totality\n\nThe root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the\nimplementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the\ncensus's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk\nreaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the\nmissing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered\ncarrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and\nexpects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it.\n\n## 2. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**\nwhen a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the\nhost order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and\nqueues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic\nconfigurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits\nbyte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure\n`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`.\n\n*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the\nnumbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,\nranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts\n(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of\ntheir members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,\nso the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the\norder in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie\nstep below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a\nfunction of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean\nisomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is\nfaithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and\ncount, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under\nwhich the members agree).\n\n*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the\nqueue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the\nsurviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to\nisomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key\ncompleteness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural.\n\n*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal\nand complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection\n`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of\ndistinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state\n(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by\nthe identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the\nmembers of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders\n`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers\nagree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the\nsame block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of\nthe procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are\nthe same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering\nall of them before the next pass changes nothing.\n\n*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is\nsorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so\nisomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\n*Termination.* Premise: the members of one guest set or map are distinct\nvalues (carrier §8 step 5, `check-abstract!`); so a passing group whose\nmembers have no unnumbered cell cannot arise (equal complete spellings\nwith every cell numbered are equal members), and every passing group\nnumbers a cell or carries one into a sub-container. There is no search\nand no re-entry: the only recursion is the key walk, over a proper\nsub-term of its caller's item, so key walks nest at most as deep as the\nstructure (`:depth`). Within one walk the measure is the pair\n`(unnumbered cells of the walk, multiset of the sizes of the queued\nmembers)`, ordered lexicographically with the multiset order on the\nsecond component, in three phases: the pre-order walk (W1–W2) is a\nfinite traversal that only numbers cells and enqueues; a W3 pass either\nnumbers a cell (first component decreases) or numbers none, in which\ncase either it changes nothing and the walk proceeds to the tie test, or\nit enters members with unique keys and re-queues the rest (each entered\nmember leaves the queue, replaced by at most the tied members of its own\nsub-containers, proper sub-terms: the multiset decreases); a tie step\nenters passing groups, and each entered member numbers a cell (first\ncomponent decreases) or is replaced in the queue by proper sub-terms of\nitself (second component decreases); a failing step ends the walk. Every\nstep decreases the measure or ends the walk, and the budget bounds the\ncount of steps.\n\nConsequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host\nenumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of\ncontainers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are\nbyte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing\nsurvives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the\ncross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before.\n\n## 3. What is refused\n\nRefused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some\nsurviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:\n\n- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose\n  own ties the key walk could not certify. A residue `[:tie K … K]`\n  records the common key and the group's size, not how the tied members\n  share cells, so equal incomplete keys need not mean isomorphic members:\n  in `#{#{[A B] [B C] [C A]} #{[D E] [F E] [D F]}}` both inner sets have\n  three members and every cell occurs twice, so both key `[:set [:tie K\n  K K]]` with the same `K`, yet a directed 3-cycle is not a 3-edge\n  orientation with a source and a sink; the state is refused and is\n  **not** interchangeable (entering the inner sets in the two host\n  orders gives different rows). `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D] [D C]}}` is\n  refused by the same clause and is interchangeable.\n- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,\n  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`\n  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream\n  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the\n  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is\n  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square\n  edge entered first give different rows, so entering tied edges in host\n  order is unsafe and this test rightly refuses it (a richer structural\n  analysis could tell a triangle from a square; this test does not try).\n- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another\n  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the\n  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`\n  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie\n  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused).\n\nEqual bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor\ndoes not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the\n2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the\nmixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the\ntwo-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a\nrefuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and\nnumbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers.\n\nMeasured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every\nlisted order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,\nelse 48 sampled orders):\n\n| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |\n| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |\n| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |\n\nEvery refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and\ndecode failures: 0 of 3300. The fourth review's independent 5,000-state\ntie-heavy corpus (its own generator, renamer and classifier; rerun by the\nauthor with the same seed, same counts):\n\n| Independent corpus | States | Numbered | Refused | exhaustively interchangeable | demonstrably not | sampled equal, undecided |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| reviewer's incidence families, 250 with install children | 5000 | 4110 | 890 | 470 | 100 | 320 |\n\nRoot or verdict-and-path mismatches over two renamed and shuffled copies\nper state: 0. \"Undecided\" means 48 sampled orders gave one root; that is\nnot a proof of interchangeability, and the 320 are reported as such. The\nrates (0.95% of random values, 2% of frame-bearing states, 17.8% of the\ntie-heavy corpus) are generator rates: no production corpus exists yet\n(no version-3 lift before 6b), and they are not evidence of practical\ncoverage either way. Valid small shapes that 6a numbers (the 2-cycle)\nrefuse under this rule; that is the accepted tradeoff of A1, a\nrefusal-capable carrier whose callers keep the live task.\n\n**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a\nfixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.\n\n## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept\n\nAgainst `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on\nlabels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation\nbound of §1.5, rewritten with an explicit stack and its `remaining`\nargument taken from the `:work` budget); inside `canonical-numbering`\n(`:236-355`): the label\nseeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`\n(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`\n(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers\n(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`\nand `:search`.\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census\n  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first, on the census row's pair\n  count, before the `cursor-cells` map (`:243`) and the labels (`:252`)\n  are built; the depth scan of §1.5 second; then body ranks and\n  occurrence counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie\n  test, the refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's\n  sections \"budgets\", \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:84-520`), charging as\n  §1.5 says. The depth scan and the estimator are the only traversals\n  that may meet unbounded nesting; both use an explicit stack of lazy\n  element sequences, never the host stack, and charge each position\n  before doing work proportional to it. Portable `.cljc` per\n  continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection; `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not\n  `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32 elements (the prototype's\n  `group-failure` uses `for`: replace); `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil\n  …)`; atoms are fine on all three hosts; the byte-payload branch of the\n  estimator keeps 6a's host conditional (`:221`). 6a's `bytes-key`\n  (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's takes a value: keep\n  6a's and encode first, after the charge.\n- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row\n  added (`[:V :V]`).\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the\n  whole `emit` call.\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),\n`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),\n`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,\n`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after\n`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,\n`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`\nkeys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`\n(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,\n`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),\n`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),\n`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`).\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on\n  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become\n  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`\n  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the\n  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the\n  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-not-spelled`: `base` (its\n  census cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of\n  §1.6 (an exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has\n  no `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit\n  base))))` (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and\n  decoding to the expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same\n  400000-element literal inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported\n  :resource-limit [:work]` under the default budget, under `{:work\n  2000}` and under `{:work 900000}`, each within a bounded number of\n  steps (§5 item 21).\n\n## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review