-gate-r3.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/archive/yin-repl/1790761307000-architect-repl-durable-index-slice2-signoff-r3.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/archive/collab/1790523000002-architect-dao-stream-remote-slice8-confirm-r3.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/archive/collab/1790596235000-reviewer-ffi-export-binding-gate-r3.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/archive/collab/1790523000002-architect-slice8-confirm-r3.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n144:  ;; then the digits. Other keys (a partial emit-numbered) sort last.\n168:(declare emit-node canonical-numbering child-numberer validate-frames)\n678:(defn canonical-numbering\n685:  ([task] (canonical-numbering task default-budget))\n686:  ([task budget] (canonical-numbering task budget (atom {})))\n701:(defn emit-numbered\n706:  ([task numbering] (emit-numbered task numbering (child-numberer default-budget)))\n718:         [id rows] (emit-node task (canonical-numbering task budget) {} [] (child-numberer budget))]\n818:(defn- decode*\n821:   Frame checks are available separately through validate-frames.\"\n822:  ([wire] (decode* wire default-budget))\n932:           (when (and (not= false (:check-numbering budget)) (not= root (:yin.k/root canonical)))\n935:             (when-let [d (validate-frames task)]\n943:  ([wire] (decode* wire default-budget))\n944:  ([wire budget] (decode* wire (dissoc budget :check-numbering))))\n952:  (let [task (decode* wire (assoc default-budget :check-numbering false))]\n1081:(defn validate-frames\n1105:                          (and (ref? d) (= :task (:kind x))) (validate-frames x)\n(ns probe.discovery\n  \"Prototype of discovery-order cursor-cell numbering, the BASE design of the\n   note of 2026-10-12 (round 4), over the 6a abstract task representation\n   (yin.vm.ucf.rows). Not repo code; a scratch project under collab/. The\n   round-3 variants (components, residue search) live in probe.discovery-r3.\n\n   Vocabulary: UCF 7.5.3. A cursor cell is the VM box the carrier serializes;\n   its source id is the lift's local name; the procedure assigns :yin.k/cc-n.\n\n   The rule. A cursor cell gets the next number the first time the walk meets\n   it. The walk is pre-order over the task in table order. A guest :set or\n   :map is turned into a vector of its members (a map entry as [:entry k v])\n   sorted by KEY: the spelling of a local walk over the member under the\n   current numbering, with numbered cells spelled by number and unnumbered\n   ones by local first-occurrence index, body rank and occurrence count.\n   Members whose keys tie are POSTPONED to the run's queue and re-keyed after\n   the rest of the walk, until a pass numbers nothing. A remaining tie group\n   is entered in listed order when the TIE TEST holds (common key complete;\n   the members' unnumbered cells pairwise disjoint and occurring in no member\n   of another remaining group); otherwise the root walk refuses\n   :yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells and a key walk spells the\n   group as [:tie K ... K] in its key.\n\n   Budgets: :cursor-cells (the census's pair count, before anything is\n   built), :depth (enforced by one bounded iterative scan of the task before\n   any recursive traversal, and again in the walk), :work (one unit per\n   position scanned, visited, keyed or spelled, plus 6a's representation\n   bound of every value before it is CBOR-encoded for a comparison, computed\n   iteratively against the remaining budget). Exceeding one is\n   :yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit, never a canonicality verdict.\n\n   Diagnostic switches (*numbering-opts*): :permissive enters a failing group\n   in listed order instead of refusing (used to test interchangeability by\n   exhaustion or sampling); :count-spelling false drops the occurrence count\n   from the key (the owner's literal direction, for measurement).\"\n  (:require [clojure.set :as set]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as rows]\n            [yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as rt]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]))\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- schema\n\n(def synthetic-table\n  \"The one row that exists only inside the procedure: a :map entry as an\n   ordered pair, so the alias pattern across key and value is in its key.\"\n  {:entry [:V :V]})\n\n(defn descriptors [kind slots]\n  (let [schema (or (get rows/row-table kind) (get synthetic-table kind))]\n    (cond (= schema :pending) (into [:D] (get rows/pending-table (first slots)))\n          (= :repeat (first schema)) (vec (repeat (count slots) (second schema)))\n          (= :pairs (first schema)) (vec (take (count slots) (cycle (rest schema))))\n          :else schema)))\n\n(defn ref? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :ref (first d))))\n(defn refs? [d] (and (vector? d) (= :refs (first d))))\n(defn v? [d] (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d))\n\n(defn bytes-key\n  \"Uncharged encoding; generators and diagnostics only.\"\n  [x] (mapv #(bit-and (int %) 255) (seq (cbor/encode x))))\n\n(defn compare-bytes [a b]\n  (loop [i 0]\n    (cond (= i (count a)) (if (= i (count b)) 0 -1)\n          (= i (count b)) 1\n          (= (nth a i) (nth b i)) (recur (inc i))\n          :else (compare (nth a i) (nth b i)))))\n\n(def keyed-structural #{:bindings :store :parked-table})\n\n(defn elem-item\n  \"The item a member is keyed and entered as: a :set member is its V; a :map\n   entry [k v] is the synthetic ordered row [:entry k v].\"\n  [kind e]\n  (if (= :map kind) [:row {:kind :entry :slots (vec e)}] e))\n\n(defn child-task? [ctx n] (and (= :task (:kind n)) (not (identical? n (:task ctx)))))\n\n;; --------------------------------------------------------------- budgets\n\n(def default-budget\n  \"6a's budget without :label-bytes and :search; :work now also counts\n   representation bytes, so its default takes over :label-bytes' scale.\"\n  (assoc (dissoc rows/default-budget :label-bytes :search) :work 4194304))\n\n(defn- limit! [ctx category]\n  (throw (ex-info \"resource limit\"\n                  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :resource-limit :yin.k/path [category]\n                   ;; probe-only diagnostic (counters at the refusal), not part of the contract\n                   :probe/used @(:counter ctx)})))\n\n(defn- spend! [ctx category amount]\n  (let [n (get (swap! (:counter ctx) update category (fnil + 0) amount) category)]\n    (when (> n (get (:budget ctx) category)) (limit! ctx category))))\n\n(defn- charge-rep-bound!\n  \"6a's conservative representation bound of x (check-label-bytes!'s `size`:\n   128 per node or scalar, 4 per character, 16 plus the length per byte\n   payload, metadata included), charged to :work. Computed with an explicit\n   stack, no recursion, against the budget REMAINING at the call: the running\n   total is checked after every node, so the estimator stops after at most\n   remaining/16 steps on any input and never finishes reading a value it\n   cannot afford. The bound is an abstract charge: an upper bound of the\n   encoding's bytes and of the encoder's intermediate nodes, not a measured\n   host-heap ceiling (the comparison vector and the encoder's intermediates\n   use more heap than the payload length). Reading x allocates the lazy\n   element sequences and `str` of names; nothing proportional to the\n   encoding.\"\n  [ctx x]\n  (let [remaining (- (get (:budget ctx) :work) (get @(:counter ctx) :work 0))]\n    (loop [stack (list (list x)) total 0 steps 0]\n      (if (empty? stack)\n        (do (swap! (:counter ctx) update :estimator-steps (fnil + 0) steps)\n            (spend! ctx :work total))\n        (let [s (first stack) stack (rest stack)]\n          (if (empty? s)\n            (recur stack total steps)\n            (let [x (first s)\n                  stack (cons (rest s) stack)\n                  n (cond (cbor/byte-payload? x) (+ 16 (alength ^bytes x))\n                          (string? x) (+ 128 (* 4 (count x)))\n                          (or (keyword? x) (symbol? x)) (+ 128 (* 4 (count (str x))))\n                          :else 128)\n                  total (+ total n)]\n              (when (> total remaining)\n                (swap! (:counter ctx) update :estimator-steps (fnil + 0) (inc steps))\n                (limit! ctx :work))\n              (recur (cond->> stack\n                       (coll? x) (cons (if (map? x) (mapcat identity x) (seq x)))\n                       (meta x) (cons (list (meta x))))\n                     total (inc steps)))))))))\n\n(defn- key-bytes!\n  \"Charge the representation bound of x to :work, then encode x for comparison.\n   Every encoding the procedure makes goes through here.\"\n  [ctx x]\n  (charge-rep-bound! ctx x)\n  (let [bs (bytes-key x)]\n    (swap! (:counter ctx) update :encoded-bytes (fnil + 0) (count bs))\n    bs))\n\n(defn- depth-scan!\n  \"The :depth gate, enforced BEFORE any recursive traversal of the task: one\n   bounded pre-order scan with an explicit stack (no recursion) over rows,\n   members, structural and literal data and the census; install children are\n   skipped (each is gated by its own procedure). The depth of a position is\n   the walk's measure, its slot steps from the task (a member step counts\n   one, a map entry's key and value two), plus, inside data, its collection\n   nesting (metadata counts one level). A position deeper than :depth refuses\n   [:depth]; every position costs one :work unit. After the scan every\n   recursive read of the task (cell scans, memo-key hashing, spelling,\n   encoding, 6a's emitter) nests at most :depth levels.\"\n  [ctx task]\n  (let [limit (get (:budget ctx) :depth)\n        node (fn [n k] [:node nil n k])\n        slot (fn [d v k] [:slot d v k])\n        data (fn [v k] [:data nil v k])]\n    ;; The stack holds lazy sequences of entries, so pushing a container's\n    ;; elements is O(1): every position is charged when it is popped, before\n    ;; any work proportional to it is done.\n    (loop [stack (list (list (node task 0)))]\n      (when (seq stack)\n        (let [s (first stack) stack (rest stack)]\n          (if (empty? s)\n            (recur stack)\n            (let [[tag d x k] (first s) stack (cons (rest s) stack)]\n              (spend! ctx :work 1)\n              (when (> k limit) (limit! ctx :depth))\n              (recur\n               (cons\n                (case tag\n                  :node\n                  (let [{:keys [kind slots]} x k' (inc k)]\n                    (if (child-task? ctx x)\n                      nil\n                      (let [ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n                        (cond\n                          (= :task kind)\n                          (mapcat (fn [i d v]\n                                    (if (contains? #{7 12} i)\n                                      (map (fn [child] (node child (inc k'))) v)\n                                      [(slot d v k')]))\n                                  (range) ds slots)\n                          (contains? keyed-structural kind)\n                          (mapcat (fn [[key v]] [(data key k') (slot (second ds) v k')]) (partition 2 slots))\n                          (= :window kind)\n                          (mapcat (fn [[r v]] [(data r k') (slot :V v k')]) (partition 2 slots))\n                          (= :set kind) (map (fn [v] (slot :V v k')) slots)\n                          (= :map kind)\n                          (mapcat (fn [[a b]] [(slot :V a (inc k')) (slot :V b (inc k'))]) (partition 2 slots))\n                          :else (map (fn [d v] (slot d v k')) ds slots)))))\n                  :slot\n                  (cond (ref? d) [(node x k)]\n                        (refs? d) (map (fn [n] (node n (inc k))) x)\n                        (= :C d) nil\n                        (v? d) (when x\n                                 (case (first x)\n                                   :cursor-cell nil\n                                   :row [(node (second x) k)]\n                                   :lit [(data (second x) k)]\n                                   [(data x k)]))\n                        :else [(data x k)])\n                  :data\n                  (concat (when (coll? x) (map (fn [e] (data e (inc k))) (if (map? x) (mapcat identity x) x)))\n                          (when (meta x) [(data (meta x) (inc k))])))\n                stack)))))))))\n\n(defn- sort-by-key!\n  \"xs in the bytewise order of (f x), each encoded once (stable).\"\n  [ctx f xs]\n  (mapv second (sort #(compare-bytes (first %1) (first %2)) (mapv (fn [x] [(key-bytes! ctx (f x)) x]) xs))))\n\n;; ------------------------------------------------------------ cell scans\n\n(defn- walk-cells\n  \"Call f on every cursor cell at a position under slot (d x); census and\n   install children excluded; one :work unit per position.\"\n# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); implemented in phase 6a (branch srvm-phase6a).** Round 4 of the same day\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\nthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\nthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\nadopted; revised the same day after the fourth review\n(`collab/1792230000000-…review-r4…findings.md`: no canonicality\ncounterexample in 5,000 states; the depth gate moved before every\nrecursive scan, the representation estimator made budget-aware, the\nprobe's decoder put in the production order, and the wording of the\ntermination measure, the incomplete-key example and the refusal figures\ncorrected). Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\ncarrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\nand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\nappendices are not.\n\n**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\nwhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\nis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\nexcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\nthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\nmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\nalready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\ncount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\nmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\nare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\nre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\nproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\nunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\nsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n\n**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\nIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\noccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\nentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\nIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\nthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\nrefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n\n## 1. The rule\n\n**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\nprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\nover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\noutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\nmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\nsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\nmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\nis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\ndescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\norder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\nare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n\n### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n\nWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\nits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\na member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\nthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\nfrom that one numbering):\n\n- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n\nKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR; the spelling is hash-free.\n`[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part\nof its key, and so is each cell's occurrence count, which separates\n`#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search (appendix A).\n\n### 1.2 The walk\n\n**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\nslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\nrepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\nwith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\nkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\nan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\nentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\nentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\nwalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\nwithin one container in key order).\n\n**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\nreceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\nis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\ncensus row stays the contents table.\n\n**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\norder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\ntreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\nre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\nqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\nremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\ntest.\n\n### 1.3 The tie test\n\nA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\nare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\nother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\ncell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\nof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\npasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\nhost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\nreturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\nresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\nthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n\nThis is a sound test, and a cheap one: (i)–(iii) are exactly what the\nproof of §2 needs for the index-matched swap of two members to be an\nautomorphism of the state; whatever it refuses may still be\ninterchangeable (§3 says what), but nothing it accepts can be host-order\ndependent. No claim is made that it is the cheapest such test, or that\nevery sound test needs a search for what it refuses.\n\n### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n\nThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\na member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\nan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\ngroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\nto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\nThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\nThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\ninstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\nthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\nby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\nresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\nsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\nnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n(`rows.cljc:576-578`), so a wrong numbering is reported as\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells` and an unresolved one as\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` even when the task also has a frame defect\n(test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, run against the rewritten\ndecoder with both carriers; the prototype's decoder reproduces this\nplacement, appendix A).\n\n### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n\n- `:cursor-cells`: the census's pair count is charged first, before the\n  census map, ranks, counts or any walk is built (6a's gate at\n  `rows.cljc:256`, kept, moved before the label preparation).\n- `:depth`: enforced second, **before any recursive traversal of the\n  task**, by one bounded iterative scan (an explicit stack, never the\n  host stack) over rows, members, structural and literal data and the\n  census, install children excluded (each is gated by its own run). The\n  depth of a position is its slot steps from the task (a member step\n  counts one, a map entry's key or value two) plus, inside data, its\n  collection nesting; a position deeper than `:depth` refuses\n  `[:depth]`. After the scan every recursive read of the task (cell\n  scans, occurrence counts, memo-key hashing, spelling, encoding) nests\n  at most `:depth` levels, so no input can overflow the host stack\n  before the gate fires: one cell inside 3000 nested vectors under\n  `{:depth 3}` refuses `[:depth]`, as does a literal nested 3000 deep.\n  The walk's own check (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`) is kept as\n  well. Default 256, unchanged.\n- `:work`: one unit per position scanned (the depth scan), visited\n  (walk, key walk, cell scans, count and rank preparation), keyed (one\n  per member per keying, so the number of key walks and memo lookups is\n  charged) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key walk's memo key, and,\n  **before every CBOR encoding made for a comparison** (keys, rank\n  labels, structural keys), the representation bound of the value\n  encoded: 6a's `size` of `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`),\n  128 per node or scalar, 4 per character, 16 plus the length per byte\n  payload, an upper bound of the encoding's bytes. The estimator is\n  iterative and budget-aware: it runs against the budget remaining at\n  the call and stops as soon as its running total exceeds it, so it\n  takes at most `remaining/16` steps on any input and never reads to the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"find /Users/sto/workspace -name 'codex-gen.clj' -o -name '*179221*findings*'; sed -n '815,958p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; rg -n 'defn|def |deftest' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc | tail -65; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"find /Users/sto/workspace -name 'codex-gen.clj' -o -name '*179221*findings*'; sed -n '815,958p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; rg -n 'defn|def |deftest' test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc | tail -65; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md\"","aggregated_output":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/collab/1792210000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/collab/numbering-probe/codex-gen.clj\n    (when (= :task (:kind root)) (check-task root []))))\n\n\n(defn- decode*\n  \"Validate gate, local schema, finite reachable graph, hashes, canonical\n   form and cursor-cell numbering, then expand structural refs. No attachment.\n   Frame checks are available separately through validate-frames.\"\n  ([wire] (decode* wire default-budget))\n  ([wire budget]\n   (let [budget (merge default-budget budget) counter (atom {})\n         rs (:yin.k/rows wire)]\n     (when-not (and (= true (:yin.k/handoff wire)) (= 3 (:yin.k/version wire))\n                    (code/reg? (:yin.k/version wire)))\n       (refuse! :yin.k/profile-mismatch :version [:yin.k/version]))\n     (when-not (and (= #{:yin.k/handoff :yin.k/version :yin.k/root :yin.k/rows} (set (keys wire)))\n                    (vector? rs) (every? #(and (vector? %) (<= 2 (count %))) rs))\n       (bad! :row-shape [:yin.k/rows]))\n     (spend! counter budget :rows (count rs))\n     (spend! counter budget :bytes (count (seq (cbor/encode wire))))\n     (let [index (into {} (map (fn [r] [(first r) r])) rs)\n           root (:yin.k/root wire)]\n       (when-not (= (count rs) (count index)) (bad! :duplicate-row [:yin.k/rows]))\n       (when-not (contains? #{:task :act :tail :wait} (second (get index root))) (bad! :root [:yin.k/root]))\n       ;; Gate children before schema, with an active path (DAG sharing is legal).\n       (letfn [(gate\n                 [id active]\n                 (when (contains? active id) (bad! :cyclic [id]))\n                 (let [row (get index id) slots (when row (subvec row 2))]\n                   (when-not (and (= :task (second row)) (= 17 (count slots))\n                                  (vector? (nth slots 12)) (every? keyword? (nth slots 12)))\n                     (bad! :task-shape [id]))\n                   (when-not (= 3 (nth slots 0)) (refuse! :yin.k/profile-mismatch :version [id 0]))\n                   (when-not (= profile (nth slots 1)) (refuse! :yin.k/profile-mismatch :profile [id 1]))\n                   (doseq [install (nth slots 12)]\n                     (let [r (get index install)]\n                       (when-not (and (= :install (second r)) (= 7 (count r))) (bad! :install-shape [install]))\n                       (gate (nth r 6) (conj active id))))))]\n         (when (= :task (second (get index root))) (gate root #{})))\n       (doseq [[id kind & slots] rs]\n         (let [schema (get row-table kind) ds (descriptors kind slots)]\n           (when-not (and schema\n                          (cond\n                            (= schema :pending) (contains? pending-table (first slots))\n                            (= :pairs (first schema)) (even? (count slots))\n                            :else true)\n                          (= (count ds) (count slots))) (bad! :row-shape [id]))\n           (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) ds slots)]\n             (when-not (structural? kind d x) (bad! :slot-kind [id i])))))\n       (let [seen (atom #{})]\n         (letfn [(walk\n                   [id active depth]\n                   (when (> depth (:depth budget)) (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:depth]))\n                   (when (contains? active id) (bad! :cyclic [id]))\n                   (when-not (contains? @seen id)\n                     (let [r (get index id)]\n                       (when-not r (bad! :missing-row [id]))\n                       (let [kind (second r) slots (subvec r 2)]\n                         (doseq [[i d x] (map vector (range) (descriptors kind slots) slots)]\n                           (let [targets (cond (ref? d) [x] (refs? d) x\n                                               (and (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d) (= :row (first x))) [(second x)]\n                                               :else [])]\n                             (doseq [target targets]\n                               (when (and (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d)\n                                          (not (contains? value-row-kinds (second (get index target)))))\n                                 (bad! :value-reference-kind [id i]))\n                               (when (and (or (ref? d) (refs? d))\n                                          (not (contains? (set (rest d)) (second (get index target)))))\n                                 (bad! :reference-kind [id i]))\n                               (walk target (conj active id) (inc depth))))))\n                       (swap! seen conj id))))]\n           (walk root #{} 0)\n           (when-not (= @seen (set (keys index))) (bad! :unreachable-row [:yin.k/rows]))))\n       (doseq [[id & body] rs]\n         (when-not (and (= :blake3 (:algorithm (jing/parse-segment-address id)))\n                        (= id (jing/segment-key (vec body))))\n           (refuse! :yin.k/hash-mismatch :row [id])))\n       (doseq [[id kind & slots] rs :when (= :task kind)]\n         (doseq [[address v] (nth slots 11)]\n           (when-not (= address (jing/segment-key v))\n             (refuse! :yin.k/hash-mismatch :code [id 11 address]))\n           (when (code/well-formed? v) (bad! :code [id 11 address]))))\n       (doseq [[_ kind & slots] rs\n               [d x] (map vector (descriptors kind slots) slots)]\n         (when-not (or (ref? d) (refs? d) (= :C d))\n           (check-literal-markers! (if (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d)\n                                     (when (= :lit (first x)) (second x)) x))))\n       (doseq [[id kind & slots] rs]\n         (when-not (= (vec slots) (ordered-slots kind (vec slots))) (bad! :row-order [id]))\n         (when (contains? #{:map :bindings :store :parked-table :window :cursor-cells :set} kind)\n           (let [keys (if (= kind :set) slots (map first (partition 2 slots)))]\n             (when-not (and (= (count keys) (count (distinct keys)))\n                            (= (count keys) (count (distinct (map #(bytes-key (cbor/encode %)) keys)))))\n               (bad! :duplicate-key [id])))))\n       (letfn [(expand-slot\n                 [d x]\n                 (cond (ref? d) (expand x) (refs? d) (mapv expand x)\n                       (and (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d) (= :row (first x))) [:row (expand (second x))]\n                       :else x))\n               (expand\n                 [id]\n                 (let [[_ kind & slots] (get index id)]\n                   (node kind (mapv expand-slot (descriptors kind slots) slots))))]\n         (let [task (expand root)\n               _ (check-abstract! task)\n               ;; Only the lift's tie refusal (root or child) is translated; a\n               ;; resource refusal propagates as itself.\n               canonical (if (= :task (:kind task))\n                           (try (emit task budget)\n                                (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n                                  (let [d (ex-data e)]\n                                    (if (and (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status d))\n                                             (= :unresolved-cursor-cells (:yin.k/kind d)))\n                                      (throw (ex-info \"Row codec refused data\"\n                                                      (assoc d :yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable\n                                                             :yin.k/path (into [root] (:yin.k/path d)))))\n                                      (throw e)))))\n                           (let [[id _] (emit-node task {} {})] {:yin.k/root id}))]\n           (when (and (not= false (:check-numbering budget)) (not= root (:yin.k/root canonical)))\n             (bad! :noncanonical-cursor-cells [root]))\n           (when (= :task (:kind task))\n             (when-let [d (validate-frames task)]\n               (throw (ex-info \"Frame validation failed\" (update d :yin.k/path #(into [root] %))))))\n           task))))))\n\n\n(defn decode\n  \"Validate and expand a row pack, including canonical cursor-cell numbering.\n   For task roots, run X1 and frame validation after numbering.\"\n  ([wire] (decode* wire default-budget))\n  ([wire budget] (decode* wire (dissoc budget :check-numbering))))\n\n\n(defn canonical-under\n  \"Test-only §5.5 re-emission under an explicit stream correspondence.\n   Missing marker coverage fails; neither traversal order nor original\n   cursor-cell numbering chooses a logical identity. Accepts a carrier.\"\n  [wire correspondence]\n  (let [task (decode* wire (assoc default-budget :check-numbering false))]\n    (letfn [(rewrite-slot\n              [d x]\n              (cond (ref? d) (rewrite x) (refs? d) (mapv rewrite x)\n                    (and (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d) (= :row (first x))) [:row (rewrite (second x))]\n                    :else x))\n            (rewrite\n134:(deftest window-sorted-and-unique\n144:(deftest canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant\n160:(deftest alias-topology-and-census\n172:(deftest symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable\n183:(deftest gates-and-graph-before-hashes\n208:(deftest numbering-precedes-frame-defect\n211:        abstract (rows/node :task (assoc slots 5 (rows/node :bindings ['yin/def [:lit 1]])))\n221:        abstract (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots cycle) 5 (rows/node :bindings ['yin/def [:lit 1]])))\n229:(deftest schema-and-literal-canonicality\n258:(deftest child-scope-and-shared-child-dag\n270:(deftest pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved\n282:(deftest task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame\n298:           (:yin.k/kind (error #(rows/decode (rows/emit (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots bad-task) 5 (rows/node :bindings ['yin/def [:lit 1]])))))))))\n303:(defn- reversed\n315:(deftest unordered-containers-are-order-free\n328:(defn- descriptors\n338:(defn- rename-and-reorder\n370:(defn- census-cells\n387:(defn- renamed-reversed\n394:(defn- verdict\n399:(defn- root-of\n404:(defn- c\n409:(defn- v\n414:(defn- s\n419:(defn- equal-bodies\n424:(defn- review-counterexample\n431:(defn- path-abc\n436:(defn- two-sets-shared\n441:(defn- two-cycle\n446:(defn- loop-and-two-cycle\n451:(defn- with-child\n458:(defn- frame-task\n484:(defn- frame-fixtures\n492:(deftest canonical-roots-are-pinned\n521:(deftest census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells\n531:(deftest marker-shaped-guest-maps-are-not-portable\n544:(deftest well-formed-guest-markers-are-refused\n552:(deftest every-literal-context-refuses-markers\n574:(deftest cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct\n582:(defn seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square\n590:(deftest seven-cursor-cells-are-refused\n600:(deftest large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-not-spelled\n642:(deftest discovery-order-numbering\n665:(deftest occurrence-count-separates-members\n674:(deftest interchangeable-ties\n688:(deftest alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key\n711:(defn- refused-states\n737:(deftest unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse\n762:(deftest numbering-is-total\n771:(deftest gates-and-work\n804:(defn- rng\n809:(defn- next-int!\n815:(defn- shuffler\n824:(defn- decoded\n838:(defn- distinct-by\n845:(defn- gen-value\n870:(defn- reached\n878:(defn- gen-task\n895:(defn- gen-frame-task\n909:(defn- gen-child-task\n918:(defn- gen-incidence-task\n946:(defn- outcome\n960:(defn- search-states\n989:(deftest counterexample-search-quick\n999:(deftest ^:slow counterexample-search\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
